1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
An architect moves a door in a hotel room.
On the plan it looks fine.
Does the swing hit anything? And which drawings must be reissued?

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:30,000
This is PLANRIFT. The room is a white study model,
driven by a real geometry engine. Drag the door along the wall.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The approved plan: door D-101 at 1,700 millimetres.
Its swing clears console F-07 by 1,083.7 millimetres.
The strip under the wall is the result at every millimetre the hinge could sit.

4
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Revision A: move the door to 3,000.
The leaf strikes the console after 15.7 degrees
and would push 188.7 millimetres into it.
Plan and 3D agree because they are drawn from one scene.

5
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Branch a second scenario from the approved plan and try 2,400.
The swing clears the console by 391.5 millimetres.

6
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:05,000
But clear is not finished. Three documents show this door:
A-101, A-401 and D-01. PLANRIFT flags them for review.
It never edits them. Room 513 is untouched.

7
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Compare the approved plan and both revisions side by side.
Submit for review, acknowledge the flagged sheets, accept.
The decision becomes a receipt.

8
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The geometry uses exact integer arithmetic.
Kiro built it from specs, steering, custom agents,
and hooks that run the tests and ask before anything goes public.

9
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
PLANRIFT. Know what a revision breaks before it is issued.
